WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.580
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:01.580
I'm gonna be completely honest.

00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:04.880
Sometimes I feel like a huge, gigantic failure. (Good start, pal.)

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.020
And we all feel like that and we're all petrified of failure. (Not me, buddy.)

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.320
But John, one thing that gets me through all of my failures

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.960
and keeps me trying new projects, day in and day out,

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.840
despite the fact that I probably will fail

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.680
is knowing that there are other people who have made decisions

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.080
that led to much bigger, much, much more significant failures (Like me.)

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.060
than I will ever even have the opportunity

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:29.160
to even get close to matching the level of those failures.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:33.400
Today, so that you and the rest of the world can know that your failures,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.960
no matter how big they seem to you, are actually insignificant,

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.020
I am going to give you a list of the

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:42.600
Top 7 most epic fails of history

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.080
that aren't in any specific order and that are actually

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:48.360
just the ones that I think are the most interesting.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.540
Let's start out in 1962, when a young four-man band walked in to Decca Studios for an audition.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:59.680
The Decca Studios manager concluded that the band had quote, "No future in show business."

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:06.180
A mere two years later, that band that we all know as the Beatles, sold 50 million dollars of records.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.700
Gigantic epic fail number 2 went a little bit like this.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:14.580
"Men, congratulations. We have been fighting a war with the Greeks for many, many years.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:20.100
They have all left, and behind, all that there is is this beautiful horse

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:25.540
that they have made us to congratulate us for our victory over them.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:31.220
Let us bring it into the walls of our city and leave it unguarded overnight."

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:32.840
Aaaaaaaah.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:37.420
Epic fail number 3. Getting involved in a land war in Asia.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.660
It's one of the classic blunders.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.880
More specifically, if you're trying to take over all of Europe,

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.720
just don't, just leave Moscow out of it. Just let them be.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:51.580
Similarly, if you are a small, though very technologically sophisticated

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:55.040
and interesting island nation, don't send a bunch of bombers over to, um,

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.380
bomb a country that you are not currently at war with.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:02.520
Especially if that country has just developed the bomb that can destroy entire cities.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.600
Sometimes it seems like these epic fails aren't being made by one person but they are,

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.780
they're decisions being made by men.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:13.500
But on a more understandable scale, we have the 1960 performance of Wim Esajas.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.380
The first Olympian from the nation of Suriname,

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:21.100
and his performance which will go down in history as the first Olympian from Suriname,

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:24.380
was to sleep through his race.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.020
Moving further back in time, in 1876,

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:31.140
Western Union owned the entire system of communication in America.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:37.320
In 1876, Gardener Green Hubbard approached Western Union with a patent for a device called the telephone.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.900
The CEO of Western Union went ahead and bypassed the agent of sale,

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.540
wrote a letter directly to Alexander Graham Bell saying that

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:47.260
Western Union had no need of this "electrical toy."

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.500
Alexander Graham Bell, instead of closing up shop,

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.060
created his own telecommunications network which he named

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.200
American Telephone and Telegraph (AT&amp;T).

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.600
The telephone became the most valuable patent in history.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.780
So tell me in the comments, despite the fact that none of your failures

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.800
have ever led to the deaths of millions of people or to the loss of billions of dollars,

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:06.200
why are we so afraid of failure? (Because I am one.)

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:09.480
And in lieu of that, what is it do you think that you're afraid of? (MYSELF, OKAY?!)

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.960
John, I’ll see you on Monday. (Stay away from me.)

